# SPDX-License-Identifier: AGPL-3.0-or-later
#
# glintfx.version - GNU ld / gold / lld linker version script (ABI-
# STDLIB-LEAK, GODS_LAWS.md L-19/L-26/L-40).
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS, NOT JUST -fvisibility=hidden: GlintfxLibrary.
# cmake already sets CXX_VISIBILITY_PRESET hidden and
# VISIBILITY_INLINES_HIDDEN ON, and every glintfx symbol correctly
# stays hidden unless marked GLINTFX_API. That command-line default is
# NOT the last word, though - libstdc++'s own headers put an EXPLICIT
# `__attribute__((visibility("default")))` on class templates like
# std::__cxx11::basic_string (bits/c++config.h's _GLIBCXX_VISIBILITY
# macro), and an explicit attribute in a header always wins over a
# compiler-flag default (documented GCC behaviour, confirmed live
# here). When one of our own translation units instantiates such a
# template and the optimizer does not fully inline every member
# function of it away, GCC emits the leftover out-of-line definition
# as a weak, DEFAULT-visibility symbol in OUR .so - regardless of
# -fvisibility=hidden - because the symbol was never ours to hide via
# that flag in the first place. Measured live, 28/08/2026: a
# CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug build (weaker inlining) exported
# std::basic_string<char>::_M_replace_cold and
# std::__detail::__from_chars_alnum_to_val_table<false>::value; the
# Release build of the SAME source happened to inline every call site
# and exported neither - an accident of optimization level, not a
# guarantee, and CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE or a future compiler version could
# make either symbol reappear in Release just as easily.
#
# THE FIX: a linker version script acts at the FINAL link, after every
# translation unit's per-TU visibility decision has already been made,
# and can force ANY symbol not on this allowlist to `local` (hidden
# from the .so's dynamic symbol table) - including one that arrived
# with header-forced default visibility. This is the same technique
# major C++ libraries (Abseil, Protobuf, Boost) use for exactly this
# problem class: -fvisibility=hidden narrows what the COMPILER decides
# to export per translation unit; a version script narrows what the
# LINKER actually publishes, and the second one cannot be overridden
# by a system header.
#
# Global patterns are Itanium-MANGLED prefixes matched directly
# against the raw symbol name, NOT wrapped in a demangled `extern
# "C++" { "glintfx::*"; }` block. GNU ld's `extern "C++" { ... }`
# construct demangles each defined symbol first and glob-matches the
# pattern against THAT demangled form ("glintfx::foo(int)", with
# argument types spelled out) - a template argument list, an
# std::string parameter written differently by two compiler versions,
# or any other demangled-spelling drift could silently stop matching
# a symbol that is still, unambiguously, ours. Matching the raw
# mangled prefix instead is exactly what
# tests/tools/check_exports.sh already does
# (EXPECTED_MANGLED_NAMESPACE="7glintfx", the "K" const-member variant
# too - see that script's CE-3 comment), so the allowlist enforced
# here at link time and the allowlist verified by the test read as
# the SAME rule written twice, not two independently-maintained ideas
# of what "public" means.
#
# UNIX/BUILD_SHARED_LIBS ONLY (cmake/GlintfxLibrary.cmake applies
# this): a version script is a GNU-linker-family construct (bfd, gold,
# lld and mold all implement --version-script; Apple's ld does not,
# but this project ships no macOS target - GODS_LAWS.md, five
# platforms, none of them macOS). It has nothing to say for a static
# archive (no linking happens building an .a) or for Windows, where
# generate_export_header's __declspec(dllexport/dllimport) already IS
# the export mechanism (tests/CMakeLists.txt's own comment on
# visibility_test says the same about the Windows side).
# Anonymous version node (no `GLINTFX_1 { ... };` tag): this project
# wants VISIBILITY control only, not symbol VERSIONING (no consumer
# needs to link against "glintfx::foo@@GLINTFX_1"; L-26's ABI story is
# SOVERSION, not per-symbol version nodes). Confirmed live, 28/08/2026:
# a NAMED node (`GLINTFX_1 { ... };`) makes GNU ld additionally emit
# its own anchor symbol - `OBJECT GLOBAL DEFAULT ABS GLINTFX_1`, size
# 0, address 0 - into the .so's dynamic symbol table, as the version
# definition's self-reference. That anchor is not one of ours, does
# not start with the mangled glintfx prefix, and check_exports.sh
# correctly flagged it as a second, DIFFERENT intruder the moment the
# first two were fixed. The anonymous form assigns every matched
# symbol to the library's base version instead of a named one and
# never allocates that anchor, closing the gap without widening the
# test's allowlist (which this task forbids touching) to special-case
# a linker implementation detail.
{
    global:
        # Free functions and non-const member functions. UNQUOTED: a
        # quoted pattern is matched LITERALLY by GNU ld (no glob
        # expansion of the trailing `*`) - confirmed live, 28/08/2026:
        # the quoted form swept every glintfx:: symbol into `local: *;`
        # right along with the two intruders, exporting nothing at
        # all. The bare, unquoted form is what actually glob-matches.
        _ZN7glintfx*;
        # Const-qualified member functions (Itanium "K" cv-infix -
        # see check_exports.sh's own CE-3 header comment for why this
        # second pattern exists as well as the first).
        _ZNK7glintfx*;
        # Minimal runtime allowlist check_exports.sh also carries
        # (L-40 finding CE-6: these are not glintfx:: symbols, but a
        # shared object may legitimately carry them from the linker's
        # own default script; keeping them global here means the gate
        # and the version script agree on the full allowed set, not
        # just the glintfx:: part of it).
        _init;
        _fini;
        _edata;
        _end;
        __bss_start;

    local:
        # Everything else - including any standard-library symbol a
        # header forces to default visibility - is hidden from the
        # dynamic symbol table. This is the line that actually closes
        # ABI-STDLIB-LEAK: it applies regardless of which header or
        # which optimization level put a symbol at default visibility
        # in the first place.
        *;
};
